  A schema is [38;5;153m{ fieldName: [rule, ...] }[39m. For login:

  [34mconst[39m loginSchema = {
    [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Username is required"[39m)],
    [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Password is required"[39m)],
  };

  [1mData flow:[0m [38;5;153mattachValidation[39m binds one [38;5;153msubmit[39m listener → [38;5;153me.preventDefault()[39m → [38;5;153mnew FormData(formEl)[39m → plain values
  object → [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m → on pass calls [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m, on fail calls [38;5;153monInvalid(errors, values)[39m. The form
  does the rest. [38;5;153mvalidate[39m never touches the DOM, so it tests as a plain function.

  [1mError shape[0m — [38;5;153m{ valid: false, errors: { username: "Username is required" } }[39m. Per-field, replacing today's single
  whole-form string, so a caller can point at the field that failed. Rules short-circuit per field: first failure wins,
  one message per field. That keeps the shape predictable; collecting all failures per field is a change we can make
  later without touching callers.

  [1mTwo behavior changes this implies, both deliberate:[0m
  1. [38;5;153mrequired[39m will reject whitespace-only input. Today [38;5;153mformData.username[39m is a falsy check, so [38;5;153m"   "[39m passes validation —
     that's a latent bug, and a shared [38;5;153mrequired[39m that keeps it would propagate it to every future form.
  2. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m needs [38;5;153mname[39m attributes on both inputs for [38;5;153mFormData[39m to see them.

  [1mOne thing this deliberately does not fix:[0m errors still go wherever the caller sends them, and in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m that stays
  [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m — invisible to the user. That follows from scoping display out, and I'd flag it as the obvious next
  piece of work rather than quietly widening this change.
